1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,742
One barrel… two fuels

2
00:00:01,742 --> 00:00:02,928
and you can never swap them

3
00:00:02,928 --> 00:00:03,407
Why?

4
00:00:03,607 --> 00:00:04,855
It all starts with crude oil

5
00:00:04,855 --> 00:00:06,999
heated to about 400°C

6
00:00:06,999 --> 00:00:08,452
until it turns into vapor

7
00:00:08,652 --> 00:00:10,174
The vapor rises up a tower

8
00:00:10,174 --> 00:00:11,838
and the higher it goes, the cooler

9
00:00:11,838 --> 00:00:13,895
Light stuff condenses up top: gasoline

10
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:15,974
Heavier stuff lower down: diesel

11
00:00:16,174 --> 00:00:18,238
In the engine, gasoline needs a spark

12
00:00:18,238 --> 00:00:19,849
from the spark plug to ignite

13
00:00:20,949 --> 00:00:22,441
Diesel needs no spark at all

14
00:00:22,441 --> 00:00:24,227
The engine squeezes air until it's hot

15
00:00:24,227 --> 00:00:26,102
and diesel ignites from the heat alone

16
00:00:26,302 --> 00:00:27,756
That's why trucks run on diesel

17
00:00:27,756 --> 00:00:29,919
Same barrel… but each needs its own engine
